<section xml:id="topic-1-7-expressions">
  <time minutes="90"/>
  <title>Mathematical Expressions</title>
   <idx>expressions</idx>
   <idx>math</idx>

  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links: 
      <xref text="global" ref="expressions, updating-state, operators, input-math, random-numbers,        coding-challenge-random-turtle-project,  vocab-1-7, appinventor-activity2"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      Computers are very good at math! In this lesson, we'll learn how to do math in Python
      using <term>mathematical expressions</term> which are mathematical formulas that combine numbers, 
      variables, and operators to calculate a value like <c>width*height</c> below. 

    <image source="_static/Unit1images/computer-math.png" width="50%" alt="computer loving math"/>

    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="expressions">
    <title>Mathematical Expressions</title>
 
    <p>In the last lesson, we assigned values to variables like <c>score = 0</c>, but the right side of an 
    assignment statement can be more than just a simple value like 0. We can use <term>expressions</term> which 
    combine numbers, variables, and operators to calculate a value and assign it to the variable in an assignment statement like below. 
    </p>
    
     <image source="_static/Unit1images/assignment.png" width="50%" alt="variable assigned an expression"/>

    <p>This looks like a mathematical formula, but in coding, an assignment statement always has a single variable on the left side and an expression on the right side. The expression is evaluated and then assigned to the variable. </p>

     <activity label="trace-expr0">
      <statement><p>If <c>points = 3</c>, what is the value of <c>score</c> after this assignment statement 
        which multiplies points by 10 and adds 5?</p>
      <program language="python">
       <code>
         score = (10 * points) + 5
       </code>
     </program>
     <p><fillin answer="35" mode="string"/></p>
     </statement>
  <evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        <feedback>
          Correct! <c>(10*3) + 5 = 35</c>
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
           Incorrect, try again. What is (10 * 3) + 5?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="updating-state"> 
  <title>Updating State with Expressions</title>
    
  <p>
      If you use a variable to keep score, you would probably <term>increment</term> the score (add
      one to the current value) as the game progresses. You can do this by
      setting the variable to the current value of the variable plus one
      (<c>score = score + 1</c>) as shown below. The formula would look strange
      in math class, but it makes sense in coding because it is assigning a new
      value to the variable on the left that comes from evaluating the
      arithmetic expression on the right. So, the score variable is set to the
      previous value of score plus 1.
    </p>

    <activity label="incrementScore">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the code below to see how score is incremented by 1. Try
          substituting 2 instead of 1 to increment by 2.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
        score = 10
        print(score)
        score = score + 1
        print(score)
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("12",self.getOutput(),"Change + 1 to + 2")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
       </program>
    </activity>


    <p>In Python, you do not need to indicate the data type of a variable, but variable that are 
      assigned whole number values like <c>score = 5</c> are automatically type <c>int</c> for <term>integer</term>.
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="operators">
    <title>Arithmetic Operators</title>

    <idx>operators</idx>
    <idx>
      <h>math</h><h>operators</h>
    </idx>

    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>addition</h>
    </idx>

    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>subtraction</h>
    </idx>

    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>multiplication</h>
    </idx>

    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>division</h>
    </idx>
    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>modulo</h>
    </idx>
    <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>mod</h>
    </idx>
     <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>remainder</h>
    </idx>
      <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>exponent</h>
    </idx>
      <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>power</h>
    </idx>
      <idx>
      <h>operators</h><h>integer division</h>
    </idx>

    <p>Python uses the following basic arithmetic operators: 
     </p>
    <table xml:id="basic-operators">
      <title>Python Basic Arithmetic Operators</title>
        <tabular>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell bottom="minor"> Operator </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Meaning </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell> +  </cell>
                <cell> Addition</cell>
            </row>
               <row>
                <cell> -  </cell>
                <cell> Subtraction</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  * </cell>
                <cell> Multiplication</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  /  </cell>
                <cell> Division </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
    </table>

    <activity label="operators">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the code below to see how the basic arithmetic operators work in Python.
          Add two more lines to the code as described.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
        print(10 + 5)
        print(10 - 5)
        print(10 * 5)
        print(10 / 5)
        # print 2 times 3

        # print 5 divided by 2
        
        </code>
         <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("6",self.getOutput(),"Check 2 times 3")
              self.assertIn("2.5",self.getOutput(),"Check 5 divided by 2")
              count = self.getEditorText().count("*")
              self.assertLessEqual(2,count, "* used")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
       </program>
      </activity>

    <p>Python also has the following non-standard operators:</p>
    <table xml:id="special-operators">
      <title>Python Special Operators</title>
        <tabular>
             <row header="yes">
                <cell bottom="minor"> Operator </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Meaning </cell>
            </row>        
            <row>
                <cell>  // </cell>
                <cell> Integer Division (everything after the decimal point is removed) </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  %  </cell>
                <cell> Modulo (integer remainder after integer division) </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell> ** </cell>
                <cell> Exponent (raises a number to a power) </cell>
            </row>

        </tabular>
    </table>

        <p>The <term>modulo (mod)</term> or <term>remainder operator</term> is very useful in coding. For example, it can be used to check if a number 
      is even (divisible by 2) if it has no remainders when divided by 2. 
      The expression <c>5 % 2</c> is equal to 1, because 5 divided by 2 is equal to 2 with a remainder of 1 
      (since 2 * 2 = 4). 
     </p>

    <activity label="special-operators">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the code below to see how the special arithmetic operators work in Python.
          Compare the result of 5/2 and 5//2 and the remainder 5%2.
          Add 1 more line to the code as described.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
        print("5 / 2 = ", 5 / 2)
        print("5 // 2 = ", 5 // 2)
        print("Mod or Remainder 5 % 2 = ", 5 % 2)
        print("5 ** 2 = ", 5 ** 2)
        # print 13 mod 5
        
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("3",self.getOutput(),"Check 13 mod 5")
              count = self.getEditorText().count("%")
              self.assertLessEqual(3,count, "% used")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
       </program>
      </activity>

  <activity label="mod-exercise">
        <statement>
            <p>What is the result of <c>2 % 6</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>0</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>This would be correct if it was <c>6 % 2</c>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>1</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>This would be correct if it was some odd number divided by 2, but it is not.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>2</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>6 goes into 2 zero times with 2 left over. If you have a smaller number divided by a larger number the remainder is always the smaller
                        number.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>6</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>If you have a smaller number divided by a larger number the remainder is always the smaller
                        number.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>

      </activity>

      <p>If you have a smaller number divided by a larger number the remainder is always the smaller
          number.</p>


    <p>If there is more than one math operator in an expression, <term>operator precedence</term> rules are used, 
      just like in math, so that multiplication *, division /, and remainder % are done before 
      addition + and subtraction -. Anything in parentheses is done first. The PEMDAS acronym 
      can help you remember the order of operations: Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division, 
      Addition and Subtraction (from left to right).
      It doesn’t hurt to put in extra parentheses if you are unsure as to what will be done first or 
      just to make it more clear.</p>

      
      <activity label="pemdas">
        <statement>
            <p>What is the result of <c>1 + 3 * (2 + 1)</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>12</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Remember PEMDAS! Don't do the addition first.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>9</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Remember to do the parentheses first.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>15</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Remember PEMDAS</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>10</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Yes, 1 + 3 * (2 + 1) = 1 + 3 * 3 = 1 + 9 = 10</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
      </activity>

       <idx>compound operators</idx>
    <p>
      Compound assignment operators <c>+=</c>, <c>-=</c>, <c>*=</c>, <c>/=</c>,
      and <c>%=</c> are shortcuts that do a math operation and assignment in one
      step. For example, <c>x += 1</c> adds 1 to the current value of <c>x</c>
      and assigns the result back to <c>x</c>. It is the same as <c>x = x +
      1</c>. This pattern is possible with any operator put in front of the
      <c>=</c> sign.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="compound-operators">
      <title>Compound Assignment Operators</title>
        <tabular>
             <row header="yes">
                <cell bottom="minor"> Operator </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Example </cell>
                <cell bottom="minor"> Equivalent to </cell>
            </row>        
            <row>
                <cell>  += </cell>
                <cell> x += 1 </cell>
                <cell> x = x + 1 </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  -= </cell>
                <cell> x -= 1 </cell>
                <cell> x = x - 1 </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  *= </cell>
                <cell> x *= 2 </cell>
                <cell> x = x * 2 </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  /= </cell>
                <cell> x /= 2 </cell>
                <cell> x = x / 2 </cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>  %= </cell>
                <cell> x %= 2 </cell>
                <cell> x = x % 2 </cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>When tracing through code with expressions, always remember to evaluate the right-hand side of the 
      assignment first, then assign the result to the variable on the left-hand side. You can practice and
    check your answers by adding <c>print</c> statements to show the variables or click on the "Show CodeLens" button
  on any Active Code exercise to turn on the interactive tracing tool called CodeLens as seen below. This is a
tool called <url href="https://pythontutor.com">Python Tutor</url> that is built into Runestone.</p>
    
    <activity label="codelensAsgn">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep clicking on the Next button at the bottom of the code to see how
          the values of the variables change as you step through the running
          program. You can see interactive tracing in any Active Code exercise by clicking the CodeLens button. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="codelens" language="python" label="codeLens1" xml:id="codelensid1">
        <code>
    score = 9;
    print(score)
    score += 1  
    print(score)
    bonus = 11 % 3
    print(bonus)
    score *= bonus
    print(score);
        </code>
      </program>
    </activity>

   <activity label="trace-expr">
        <statement>
            <p>What are the values of x and y after the following code executes?
              Remember to evaluate the right hand side of = first and then assign the result to the variable on the left side.
          You can step through this code by clicking on this <url href="https://pythontutor.com/visualize.html#code=x%20%3D%202%0Ay%20%3D%2010%0Ax%20%3D%20y%0Ax%20%3D%20x%20%2B%201%0Ay%20%3D%20y%20*%203&amp;curInstr=5&amp;mode=display&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js\&amp;py=311"> visualizer link</url>.</p>
          <program language="python">
            <code>
            a = 2
            b = 10
            a = b
            a = a + 1
            b = b * 3
            </code>
          </program>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 10, b = 30</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Remember that a is incremented.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 11, b = 33</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>No, b's value is copied into a but they are not connected after that.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 11, b = 30</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct!</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>a = 10, b = 33</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Try the visualizer to see the step-by-step evaluation.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
      </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="input-math">
    <title>Input and Math</title>
    <idx>input</idx>
    
        <p>Many algorithms that use calculations follow a simple pattern:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><term>Input:</term> Get input from the user and save it into variables.</li> 
      <li><term>Calculate:</term> Calculate the result based on the input.</li> 
      <li><term>Output:</term> Print the result.</li> 
    </ol>


      <activity label="parsons-algorithm-input-math" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show"
      language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put the following code into the correct order. The code should compute and print the salary
          based on the hours worked and the hourly pay rate provided by the user. Notice how the input
          is converted to the int or float data type so that it can be used in the calculation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block>
          <cline>hoursWorked = int(input("How many hours did you work? "))</cline>
          <cline>hourlyRate = float(input("What is your hourly rate? "))</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>salary = hoursWorked * hourlyRate</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>print("Your salary is:", salary)</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

    <p>When you use the <c>input()</c> command to get input from a user, the value is always treated as a string, 
      even if the user types in a number. If you want to use that input in a mathematical expression, you need to 
      convert it to a number first using the <c>int()</c> command for integers or <c>float()</c> command for 
      decimal numbers. For example:</p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
ageStr = input("How old are you? ")
age = int(ageStr)
# Now we can do math with the age variable since it is an int instead of a string
print("Next year you will be", age + 1, "years old.")
        </code>
     </program>

         <p>Or you can get the input and convert it in one line by embedding the input in the conversion function:</p>
     <program language="python">
        <code>
# Get the age from the user and convert it to an int in one line
age = int(input("How old are you? "))
</code>
</program>

      <activity label="input-math">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try the code below to see that there is an error when you try to do math with a string. 
          Then, fix the code by converting the input to an int before doing the math.
        </p>
      </statement>
    <program language="python" interactive="activecode">
        <code>
        ageStr = input("How old are you? ")
        # To fix the error, convert the string ageStr to an int and use it in the formula below
        yearsUntil100 = 100 - ageStr
        print("You will be 100 in", yearsUntil100, "years.")
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("You will be 100",self.getOutput(),"It works!")
              self.assertIn("int(",self.getEditorText(),"Convert input to int")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <idx>format</idx>
    <idx>round</idx>

    <p>Let's practice getting input, converting it, and using it in a calculation below. The
      code below also demonstrates how to format a number to 2 decimal places using the 
      <c>format(num, ".2f")</c> command. This function will also round the number to 2 decimal places if it has more than 2 decimal places.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
        <code>
# formatting a variable to 2 float or decimal places
cost = 10.666666
print("Cost is $", format(cost, ".2f"))
        </code>
     </program>

    <activity label="calculate-quantity-cost">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the code below, ask the user for how many pieces of candy they want to buy. 
          Assume that each piece of candy costs $0.25. Then, calculate the total cost and print it out.
        </p>
      </statement>
    <program language="python" interactive="activecode">
        <code>
candyCost = 0.25
# Ask the user how many pieces of candy they want to buy
# Don't forget to convert it to an int!
candyQuantity = 

# Compute the total cost of the candy they want to buy
totalCost =

# Print the total cost formatted with 2 float or decimal places
print("The total cost is $", format(totalCost, ".2f"))
        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("input(",self.getEditorText(),"Use input function")
        self.assertIn("int(",self.getEditorText(),"Convert input to int")
        self.assertIn("*",self.getEditorText(),"Use candyQuantity * candyCost to calculate totalCost")

myTests().main()
         </tests>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>In order to compute the number of hours in a given number of minutes, you can divide the number of minutes by 60, since
      there are 60 minutes in an hour. The remainder operator <c>%</c> can be used to calculate the remaining minutes after the hours are calculated. 
      For example, if there are 150 minutes, then that is 2 hours and a remainder of 30 minutes.
    </p>

<activity label="mixedUpMath" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>The following program segment should calculate the number of hours and then the remaining 
          minutes in 150 minutes, but the blocks are mixed up. 
          For example, if there are 150 minutes, then that is 2 hours
          and a remainder of 30 minutes.  Remember that <c>//</c> in Python is integer division
          that returns a whole number, <c>/</c> is float division, and <c>%</c> is the remainder operator. 
          Drag the blocks from the left and put them in the correct order on the right. 
          There will be 2 extra blocks that you will not use. 
          Click the <em>Check Me</em> button to check your solution.</p>
      </statement>
      <blocks>
        <block>
         <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>minutes = 150</cline>
          </choice>
        </block>
       
        <block>
          <choice correct="yes">
           <cline>hours = minutes // 60</cline>
          </choice>
          <choice>
           <cline>hours = minutes / 60</cline>
          </choice>
        </block>
        <block>
          <choice correct="yes">
           <cline>remainingMinutes = minutes % 60</cline>
          </choice>
          <choice>
           <cline>remainingMinutes = minutes / 60</cline>
          </choice>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

    <activity label="calculate-hours">
