        </program>
    </activity>
    </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="random-numbers">
    <title>Random Numbers</title>
    <idx>random numbers</idx>
    <idx>random</idx>

    <p>Games would be boring if the same thing happened each time you played the
      game. Random numbers can be used in games to generate different actions or outcomes
      each time the game is played. Try a game app on your phone or computer and try to identify where
      random numbers are used. If you roll dice or flip a coin or if the enemies in the game do not always act 
    the same way, then random numbers are likely being used.</p>

    <p>In Python, there is a library called <c>random</c> that can be imported and used to generate 
    random values. Use <c>random.randint(start, end)</c> to generate a random integer from <c>start</c> to 
    <c>end</c>, including both endpoints. For example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
import random
# pick a random number from 0 to 10
r = random.randint(0, 10)
      </code>
    </program>
    
    
    <p>Try running the program below a few times to see that it generates different
    random numbers each time it runs.</p>
  <activity label="random-num-gen">
    <statement>
      <p>Try the code below to see how to generate random numbers in Python. 
        Run it a few times to see that it generates different random numbers each time.
      Then, generate another random number from -10 to 10.</p>
    </statement>
  <program xml:id="random-num-generator" interactive="activecode" language="python">
    <code>
import random
# pick a random number from 1 to 100 
r = random.randint(1, 100)  
print(r)
# print another random number from -10 to 10
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              count = self.getEditorText().count("randint")
              self.assertLessEqual(2,count, "randint used twice")

          myTests().main()
         </tests>
  </program>
</activity>

    <p>Computers can’t usually generate truly random numbers without measuring something that is
      random (like static noise on radio frequencies or lava lamps). If they don’t have access to something like
      that, they must rely on generating <term>pseudorandom</term> numbers that look
      random even though they are created using a mathematical formula. Python takes the system
      time as the seed or starting point (since it is always changing) and then uses a mathematical formula to
      generate the next random number each time we ask for one.</p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="coding-challenge-random-turtle-project">
    <title>Coding Challenge: Random Turtle Project</title>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>random turtle</h></idx>
    <idx><h>coding challenge</h><h>random turtle</h></idx>
    
    <p>
      Let's practice with turtles drawing with random colors and random movements. 
      Colors on computers and on the web can be set using RGB (red, green, blue) values that range from 0 to 255 (which can be saved in 8 bits). 
      Try the <url href="https://www.w3schools.com/colors/colors_picker.asp">Color Picker</url> and look at the RGB values for different colors
      below the selected color box.  Any color can be made by mixing different amounts of red, green, and blue light. Mixing light is 
      different than mixing paint, so when you mix red, green, and blue light, you get white light.
    </p>
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/RGB.png" width="40%" alt="Mixing red, green, and blue light to make colors"/>

    <activity label="rgb-color">
     <statement> <p>What RGB values do you need to make black? Try the <url href="https://www.google.com/search?q=colorpicker">Google color picker</url> to test.</p></statement>
     <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>0, 0, 0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! Black light is the default state of no light. For example at nighttime, all is black because of there is no sunlight.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>255, 255, 255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is white, not black. If you mix all three primary RGB colors of light, you get white.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>255, 0, 0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is red light, not black. Red light is made with all red and no green or blue.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>0, 255, 255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>This is cyan light, not black. Cyan light is made with all green and blue and no red.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
     </choices>
    </activity>

    <activity label="rand-predict">
     <statement> <p>The assignment statement <c>x = random.randint(-200, 200)</c> can be used to generate a random x-coordinate for a turtle,
      because it is initially placed at coordinates (0, 0) and can move 200 pixels to the right to 200 or left to -200. 
      Which of the following numbers cannot be generated by this expression?</p></statement>
     <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>0</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>0 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>-200</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>-200 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
            <statement>
                <p>200</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>200 is between -200 and 200, so it can be generated.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>-255</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>-255 is not between -200 and 200, so it cannot be generated by random.randint(-200, 200).</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
     </choices>
    </activity>

       <project label="coding-challenge-random-turtle">
      <title>Random Turtle Project</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the code below, you will use random numbers to set the background color, 
          pen color, and turtle coordinates. Right now it uses default colors.
          Change the lines of code that say "# Change these to use random.randint". 
          Also calculate the distance that the turtle has traveled.
          Run the code a few times to see that it generates different random colors and movements each time it runs. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    <program xml:id="random-turtle" interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
import turtle
import random
screen = turtle.Screen()
turt = turtle.Turtle()
turt.shape("turtle")
# total_distance keeps track of the total distance the turtle has traveled
total_distance = 0
# Do not repeat the code above

# Repeat code from here
# Choose random RGB values for a light background color from 150 to 255
# You can choose different ranges within that for r, g, and b.
r = 255    # TODO: Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
g = 255
b = 255
# Set the background color using the random RGB values (scaled by /255)
screen.bgcolor(r/255, g/255, b/255)  

# Choose random RGB values for a dark pen color from 0 to 255 for 2 of the colors
# and 0 to 100 for the other color to make it darker.
r = 0    # TODO: Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
g = 0
b = 0
# Set the pen color using the random RGB values (scaled by / 255)
turt.color(r/255, g/255, b/255)  

# Choose a random x and y coordinate from -200 to 200
x = 100   # TODO: Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
y = 100
turt.begin_fill()  # Start filling the shape with the pen color
turt.goto(x, y)  # Move the turtle to the random (x, y) coordinates
# distance from the origin (0, 0) to the random (x, y) coordinates
distance_from_origin = turt.distance(0, 0)

# Choose a random angle from 80 to 100 and a random distance from 50 to 100
angle = 45   # TODO: Change these to use random.randint(start,end)
distance = 50
turt.right(angle)       # Turn the turtle right by the random angle
turt.forward(distance)  # Move the turtle forward by the random distance
turt.goto(0, 0)         # Go back to the origin (0, 0)
turt.end_fill()         # Stop filling the shape with the pen color

# TODO: Add to total_distance: (2 times distance_from_origin) + distance

print("Total distance: ", format(total_distance, ".2f"))

# TODO: Copy the code from line 10 to 45 (or whatever line the print is on) and paste below two more times 

      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def findLine(self, lines, target):
                  results = []
                  for line in lines:
                      if "#" in line:
                          index = line.find("#")
                          line = line[0:index]
                      if target in line:
                          #print(line)
                          results.append(line)
                  return list(set(results))
              
              def remove_comments(self, code):
                  lines = code.split("\n")
                  new_lines = []
                  for line in lines:
                      if "#" in line:
                          line = line.split("#")[0]
                      new_lines.append(line)
                  return "\n".join(new_lines)

              def test1(self):
                  code = self.getEditorText()
                  code = self.remove_comments(code)
                  code = code.replace(" ", "")

                  count = code.count("randint")
                  self.assertLessEqual(30,count, "at least 30 randint used")

                  # background color
                  count = code.count("=random.randint(150,255)")
                  self.assertLessEqual(9,count, "randint(150, 255) used to determine background colors")

                  # dark pen color
                  count = code.count("=random.randint(0,100)")
                  count += code.count("=random.randint(0,255)")
                  self.assertLessEqual(9,count, "randint(0, 100) used to determine dark pen colors")

                  # distance
                  count = code.count("=random.randint(-200,200)")
                  self.assertLessEqual(6,count, "randint(-200, 200) used to determine x and y coordinates")

                  # angle
                  count = code.count("angle=random.randint(80,100)")
                  self.assertLessEqual(3,count, "randint(80, 100) used to determine angles")

                  # distance
                  count = code.count("distance=random.randint(50,100)")
                  self.assertLessEqual(3,count, "randint(50, 100) used to determine distances")

                  # distance
                  #count = code.count("total_distance+=2*distance_from_origin+distance")
                  lines = self.findLine(code.split("\n"), "total_distance")
                  for i in range(len(lines)-1, -1, -1):
                      line = lines[i]
                      if "=0" in line or "print" in line:
                          print(lines[i])
                          lines.pop(i)

                  if len(lines) == 0:
                      self.assertTrue(False, "total_distance calculated correctly 3 times (total_distance += 2*distance_from_origin + distance) and added to total_distance")
                  else:
                      line = lines[0]

                      print(line)

                      hasMult = "*" in line
                      hasAdd = line.count("+") >= 1
                      hasDistanceFromOrigin = "distance_from_origin" in line
                      passes = hasMult and hasAdd and hasDistanceFromOrigin

                      self.assertTrue(passes, "total_distance calculated correctly 3 times (total_distance += 2*distance_from_origin + distance) and added to total_distance")

                  # printing distance
                  output = self.getOutput()
                  count = output.count("Total distance:")
                  self.assertLessEqual(3,count, "\"Total distance:\" printed three times")
                
          myTests().main()
       </tests>
    </program>
    </project>

